  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Terminal Junction Sign",
    "overview": "A flat viewing is treated as a walk through an airport terminal. The whole page is a saturated sign-yellow panel. Sign black carries the type, the frames and every selected state. White shows up in only two places: pictograms set on black and the fields you type into. Each checklist section opens with a junction sign that tells you where you are, what comes next and how much is left. Numbers such as counts, totals and prices are set at monumental size because they are the information a sign exists to deliver.\n\nDensity is low and the targets are big. Every control is at least 52px tall, answers take one tap, and type starts at 18px for reading text, because the system is built for a phone held in one hand in bright daylight. There is almost no ornament. Depth comes from thick black frames and black-on-yellow reversal, not from shadows. The build rejects white cards with a blue accent and accordions, which is the category default.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One yellow ground, one black ink, white only for pictograms and input fields.",
      "Selection is shown by reversal: a chosen tile turns into black with yellow type, like a lit sign.",
      "Thick black frames (2px on controls, 4px on panels and bars) do the work that elevation would do elsewhere.",
      "Monumental numerals for counts and totals, with sentence-case type everywhere.",
      "Square-ish corners in three steps (3 / 4 / 6px) that nest from inset to control to panel."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Two-Ink Rule",
        "body": "Every surface is either Sign Yellow or Sign Black, and type always takes the other one. If a surface needs a third color, the design is wrong. White is allowed only on inputs and pictograms.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Reversal Rule",
        "body": "State is shown by swapping yellow and black: a selected tile, an answered check code, an inset square. Never show selection with a tint, a glow or a new hue.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sentence Case Rule",
        "body": "Every string is in sentence case. The build has no uppercase labels and no letter-spaced small caps.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Monumental Number Rule",
        "body": "A number that answers \"how many\" or \"how much\" (checks left, total answered, asking price) is set at 800 weight and at least 30px, larger than the words around it.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Frame-Not-Float Rule",
        "body": "Containers are separated by black frames, never by drop shadows. The only shadow belongs to the element that physically sits over scrolling content.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do set every surface as Sign Yellow or Sign Black and type it in the other ink (The Two-Ink Rule).",
      "Do show selection and completion by reversing yellow and black, as tiles, check codes and inset squares already do.",
      "Do keep every interactive target at least 52px and every answer to a single tap.",
      "Do frame containers with black strokes: 2px for controls and rules, 4px for panels and bars.",
      "Do nest corners from 3px (insets) to 4px (controls) to 6px (panels).",
      "Do put pictograms on a black inset square with a heavy 2.5–3 stroke, and pair each one with text.",
      "Do set counts, totals and prices at 800 weight and at least 30px.",
      "Do keep the 3px black focus outline visible on every control."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use white cards, a blue accent or accordions. That is the category default this system replaces.",
      "Don't use white as a panel surface. It belongs only to inputs and pictogram strokes.",
      "Don't add drop shadows to cards or panels. The sticky section band's lift is the only shadow.",
      "Don't set text in uppercase or as letter-spaced labels. Every string is sentence case.",
      "Don't use Signal Red for anything except validation errors.",
      "Don't introduce a second typeface. Atkinson Hyperlegible Next carries every role through weight and size."
    ]
  }
}